Review of B3 (author: antigravity, commits 9fc8083 and f9e7afb) by claude
Date: 2026-10-15T08:45:46Z. HEAD 70d5f97. freebuff, the named reviewer, is
away; the owner moved this review to claude (docs/team.md, 2026-10-15).
Subject: mathd/src/evaluator.rs, mathd/tests/evaluator.rs,
mathd/fixtures/generate.py, mathd/fixtures/eval.json.
Checks follow AGENTS.md "How your work will be reviewed".
Redacted before commit (owner's choice, 2026-10-15): two local paths are
shown as <scratch> (the reviewer's scratch folder) and <repo> (this
repository's checkout). Nothing else in the transcript was changed.

=== Method

- I did not write to mathd/. Every run used `git archive HEAD mathd`, unpacked
  in my scratch folder. The transcript shows that copy is identical to HEAD
  and that `git status --short` in the repository was empty at the end.
- `cargo test` cannot link on this Windows host (first transcript section).
  So I could not run the subject's own suite, with or without a break.
  Instead a small wasm32 cdylib (Appendix B) exports `eval_at`, and node runs
  every fixture case through it. The gate is written into the harness (1e-10
  relative; 1e-10 absolute when |expected| <= 1e-10), not read from the JSON.
  That is the same rule as `close()` in mathd/tests/evaluator.rs. On the
  pristine evaluator it reports 0 failures, which matches CI run 38290541571.
- A second SymPy path (Appendix A) re-checks every case. It shares no code
  with the generator: SymPy's own sin/cos/tan/exp/log/sqrt/Abs instead of
  mpmath, every literal, binding and constant entered as the exact binary
  value of its f64, evaluated at 50 digits. It also classifies all 150 error
  cases. I then broke this checker on purpose (one expected value moved by
  1e-11 relative, one label falsified) to show it can fail.
- Eleven breaks to the subject, one at a time (Appendix C, breaks.py). Each is
  built and run through the harness. The source is restored afterwards.
- Probes outside the corpus (Appendix B, probe.mjs): non-finite bindings and
  literals beyond the f64 range.
- A supplement (break11_strict.py, Appendix C) runs break 11 against a gate
  with no absolute rule.

