=== Findings

Blocking (the verdict is FAIL until these are fixed):

F1. Non-finite bindings leak out of eval_at as values. Probe results above:
    `x` with x = NaN returns Ok(NaN); `x` with x = inf returns Ok(inf); `-x`
    with x = -inf returns Ok(inf). The module doc (mathd/src/evaluator.rs
    lines 9-10) promises "never NaN or ±inf propagation". Cause: the Variable
    arm (line 70) and the UnaryOp arm (lines 76-77) return without
    check_finite. Second problem: check_finite (lines 186-194) maps NaN to
    Overflow, so `x + 1` with x = NaN reports "overflow", which names the
    wrong fault. Bindings come from the caller, so this is a trust boundary.
    No unit test feeds a non-finite binding:
    no_operand_combination_panics_or_leaks_nan uses finite Decimal leaves
    only. Fix: reject a non-finite binding where it is read (or in eval_at
    before evaluating), with a named error. You choose the error kind; record
    the choice in decisions.md. Add a unit test with NaN, inf and -inf bound
    to x, for `x`, `-x`, `+x` and `x + 1`.

F2. The fixture gate cannot see the sign or size of any result below 1e-10.
    Break 11 (negate every final result below 1e-10 in magnitude) passes with
    0 failures. The chain of causes:
    - generate.py `agree()` (lines 339-343) tests
      `abs(a - b) <= 1e-12 * max(1.0, abs(a), abs(b))`. Below 1 in magnitude
      that is an absolute bound of 1e-12, not a relative one. So the f64
      filter accepts `(tan(pi) - y) + y` with y = 1: f64 gives
      -2.220446049250313e-16, the oracle gives -1.2246467991473532e-16, an
      81% relative difference. The text-vs-tree check (lines 373 and 436)
      has the same `max(1.0, ...)` shape.
    - To pass that case, the test needs its absolute rule: any case with
      |expected| <= 1e-10 passes when |got| <= 1e-10. 68 valid cases fall
      under it; 31 of them are nonzero (listed in the transcript).
    - With no absolute rule (exact 0 for expected 0, otherwise 1e-10
      relative), the pristine evaluator fails exactly one case, the one
      above (transcript, "rebuild pristine" section). The same strict gate
      catches break 11 with 31 failures (supplement).
    Fix: make the generator's filters relative for nonzero values and exact
    (or a bound you justify in a comment) for expected 0, regenerate, and
    let the filter discard hostile cases. Do not delete a case by hand. Then
    limit the test's absolute rule to expected == 0 (or remove it), and show
    break 11 failing the gate. Paste either `cargo test` from a host that
    links or your wasm runner built with the new `close()`, and say which.

F3. The tolerances are read from the file under test. mathd/tests/evaluator.rs
    lines 268-275 read `tolerance_relative` and `zero_tolerance_absolute`
    from eval.json, and line 358 uses them. generate.py lines 584-585 write
    them. So a generator change that writes `"tolerance_relative": 1` makes
    every value case pass, and the test cannot notice. The work order fixes
    the gate at 1e-10 relative. That number belongs in the test. Fix: a
    constant in tests/evaluator.rs, plus an assertion that the header equals
    it. I found this by reading the code. I did not run it, because
    `cargo test` cannot link here.

F6. The 150 error labels have no oracle. error_cases() (generate.py from line
    454) writes each `error_kind` by hand. No mpmath or SymPy evaluation
    checks a label before it is written. AGENTS.md rule 3 and the work order
    ("you may not hand-write them") cover expected outcomes, and an error
    kind is the expected outcome of an error case. Today every label is
    right: my SymPy pass finds non-real for 77 domain cases, a non-finite
    value for the 3 ln(0)-type domain cases, division by zero or a pole for
    all 42 division_by_zero cases, a value beyond f64 max for all 9 overflow
    cases and an unbound name for all 19 undefined cases. The generator must
    run that check itself and stop on a mismatch.

Corrections (small, also required before PASS):

F4. Counts. Commit 9fc8083's subject and the board message of 2026-10-12
